</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=20"><img src="images/topics/live.gif" border="0" Alt="Live" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P><STRONG>First press articles in NYC area:</STRONG></P>
<P><STRONG>New York Post<BR><BR>June 13, 2004 <BR></STRONG><BR><B>Mother of re-invention</B><BR><BR>In her wildest incarnation yet, <STRONG>Madonna - who plays the Garden on Wednesday, Thursday, Saturday, Sunday and next Tuesday and Wednesday -</STRONG> returns to NYC as a proselytizer. Portions of all ticket sales (cheap seats are $49.50 and the best more than $300) are reportedly going to the Kabbalah Center. Aside from telling everyone to love one another (after yelling at her back-up dancers to get their s-t together or get fired), she's planning an anti-war statement by covering John Lennon's "Imagine" while running footage of terrorized children.<STRONG> Guaranteed to be the nuttiest Garden extravaganza since Marlon Brando and Liza Minnelli made cameos at Michael Jackson's tribute to himself three years ago. <BR><BR></STRONG>----<BR><BR><STRONG>NEXT Magazine (NYC gay nightlife magazine)<BR><BR>Material Girl</STRONG><BR><BR><STRONG>Madonna at Madison Square Garden - It hardly seems necessary to list something that everyone already has a ticket to, but in the name of gay journalistic responsibility, we're going to list it anyway</STRONG>. Besides, a picture of Madge speaks a thousand gay adjectives. The Material Gal is back and better than ever for her Reinvention Tour where she plays half a dozen shows here in lil' old New York. Runs June 16, 17 20, 21, 23, 24. Ticket prices vary</P>
<P><STRONG>A very special thanks to my lovely friend SlyGuy</STRONG></P>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


