<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=28"><img src="images/topics/awards.gif" border="0" Alt="Awards" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><STRONG>Madonna's house in order<BR><BR></STRONG>by Phil Kemp<BR><BR><BR>It's that time of year again. <BR><BR>The time for the big one, the most accurate reading on the cultural barometer.<BR><BR><STRONG>Yes, it's the 2004 fashion awards of Good Housekeeping magazine.<BR></STRONG><BR>Comes round so soon, doesn't it?<BR><BR><B>And the winner is, in the which-lady-scrubs-up-best-even-though-she's-over-40-and-American-and-this-is-Britain category, is...Madonna.<BR><BR>Bet Mrs Ritchie'll treat herself to another four-hour, high-octane session of Kabbalah brain-elevating transcendentalism or something to celebrate.<BR><BR><BR>A honcho from the supermarket rag explained to the Times before we had time to switch off and read something else: "The great thing about Madonna is that she constantly looks good and constantly re-invents herself and sets trends."</B><BR><BR>Well spotted.<BR><BR>High among the tut-tuts and finger-wags dished out by GH pollsters goes to bits-showing ballsy bawler, Christina Aguilera, who, falls into the "should know better" fashion category, along with the odious Trinny and Susannah of sneery-snooty-telly-fash fame, plus Nigella, Meg 'remember her?' Mathews, Sven's Nancy and Linda 'Snip Snip' Barker, the stencil-happy flesh-flasher of the D-list DIY scene.<BR><BR>Tomorrow, June Whitfield and Des O'Connor duet on the Acker Bilk remix of Lady Marmalade at the People's Friend Awards, Poppe Inne Tearooms, Swanage. <BR><BR><BR>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


